1
00:00:17,990 --> 00:00:20,230
 Children, you'll go to high school, hmm?

2
00:00:20,230 --> 00:00:21,420
 Yeah, oh yeah.

3
00:00:21,420 --> 00:00:26,610
 Hmm, they'll teach you what man has learned,

4
00:00:26,610 --> 00:00:32,110
 the accumulated knowledge of hundreds of thousands of years.

5
00:00:32,110 --> 00:00:35,990
 Know who first thought of the idea of high school?

6
00:00:35,990 --> 00:00:38,890
 So, uh, the Department of Education.

7
00:00:38,890 --> 00:00:40,430
 It's Charlemagne.

8
00:00:40,430 --> 00:00:42,550
 It's grown-ups who thought up high schools

9
00:00:42,550 --> 00:00:46,310
 to give us a hard time.

10
00:00:46,310 --> 00:00:50,200
 Oh, you're all wrong, it's Aristotle.

11
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:52,970
 Aristotle's a strange name, was he Greek?

12
00:00:52,970 --> 00:00:54,510
 Yeah, that's what he was, a Greek

13
00:00:54,510 --> 00:00:56,930
 and one of the most brilliant.

14
00:00:56,930 --> 00:00:59,550
 Well, yeah, it was, well, let's see.

15
00:00:59,550 --> 00:01:03,010
 It was more than 23 centuries ago now, in Athens.

16
00:01:03,010 --> 00:01:08,110
 Look here. I have decided to establish a high school.

17
00:01:08,110 --> 00:01:12,410
 Young people will attend and be taught geography and geometry and history.

18
00:01:12,410 --> 00:01:15,450
 No, Master, they won't attend. They'll think it's boring.

19
00:01:15,450 --> 00:01:17,570
 Yes, of course they'll come.

20
00:01:17,570 --> 00:01:20,870
 Because they'll be learning in an amusing way walking about.

21
00:01:20,870 --> 00:01:24,990
 And today, such a school based on Aristotle's ideas is called peripatetic.

22
00:01:24,990 --> 00:01:26,750
 Peripatty what?

23
00:01:26,750 --> 00:01:33,990
 Peripatetic. That means everybody walking about because his students weren't stuck sitting in one place.

24
00:01:33,990 --> 00:01:35,870
 They would learn walking about.

25
00:01:35,870 --> 00:01:38,720
 That's a great idea. Why can't we do that?

26
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:59,900
 Taddle was also the main teacher of Alexander the Great who conquered the world.

27
00:01:59,900 --> 00:02:04,000
 Dolomni, construct a great port here, and there on the island a light.

28
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:12,180
 Speak into sailors on the sea. Up there near the port, you will have a temple erected in honor of the Muses,

29
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:14,650
 where scientists will meet and work together.

30
00:02:14,650 --> 00:02:19,340
 Uh, so be it, Alexander, and we shall call it Alexandria.

31
00:02:19,340 --> 00:02:34,330
 Ptolemy succeeded Alexander and built a great library and his son Ptolemy II a lighthouse 40 meters high, one of the wonders of the world. Extraordinarily, large ships were constructed. Just imagine, the mast was 10 stories high, and there were 2,000 oarsmen.

32
00:02:34,330 --> 00:03:25,500
 Sure, she's the biggest, but no port is wide enough nor deep enough to accommodate her.

33
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:33,150
 The most extraordinary achievement of all was the building of the great library of Alexandria.

34
00:03:33,150 --> 00:03:39,580
 Demetrius, this place is supposed to be the VBL.

35
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:41,410
 The VBL, Majesty?

36
00:03:41,410 --> 00:03:45,190
 Library, but look around.

37
00:03:45,190 --> 00:03:47,360
 Oh, what do you mean?

38
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:49,550
 What's supposed to be in a library?

39
00:03:49,550 --> 00:03:52,890
 Uh, I got it, a book, sir.

40
00:03:52,890 --> 00:03:54,200
 Just so.

41
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:57,980
 Hey, then, do you see any?

42
00:03:57,980 --> 00:04:03,740
 The text that we have is this, by Plato.

43
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:07,330
 And it's not even an original, it's a copy.

44
00:04:07,330 --> 00:04:11,370
 Look here, Demetrius, we need others. Get them any way you can.

45
00:04:11,370 --> 00:04:13,210
 Any way I can, Majesty.

46
00:04:14,810 --> 00:04:20,980
 Xenodatus, you will board that ship. It's going to Athens.

47
00:04:20,980 --> 00:04:24,610
 You will obtain some of Aristoteles' original texts.

48
00:04:24,610 --> 00:04:28,510
 Then you will go to Progamus and bring back texts by Hippocrates.

49
00:04:28,510 --> 00:04:31,270
 Meanwhile, we will inspect each and every ship.

50
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:41,660
 What's so funny? What are you laughing at? Tell me.

51
00:04:41,660 --> 00:04:45,660
 A comedy by Aristophanes. It's really good. The Birds, it's called.

52
00:04:45,660 --> 00:04:51,010
 By order of King Ptolemy, I requisitioned this text to make it available for study at the VBL.

53
00:04:51,010 --> 00:04:52,670
 The what?

54
00:04:52,670 --> 00:04:54,670
 The VBL. The very big library.

55
00:04:54,670 --> 00:04:56,640
 Yes, all right. You will return it.

56
00:04:56,640 --> 00:05:08,560
 Yes, right. And on time.

57
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:14,580
 Well, you have a copy of The Epidemics by Hippocrates.

58
00:05:14,580 --> 00:05:19,970
 Um, I have the original.

59
00:05:19,970 --> 00:05:21,350
 I'll take it.

60
00:05:21,350 --> 00:05:24,760
 But I won't sell it. I inherit it from my grandfather.

61
00:05:24,760 --> 00:05:26,760
 Give you ten talents in gold.

62
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:33,460
 Pretty golden talents.

63
00:05:33,460 --> 00:05:35,460
 I wouldn't part with it for anything in the world, but...

64
00:05:35,460 --> 00:05:36,460
 Say 30 talents?

65
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:37,460
 Go get it.

66
00:05:37,460 --> 00:05:43,790
 Here's your gold.

67
00:05:43,790 --> 00:05:48,010
 Assure you, Xenodotis, I have no need of money.

68
00:05:48,290 --> 00:05:52,470
 Tell Demetrius I have no intention of giving up my original text by democracies.

69
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:54,370
 The answer is no, definitely.

70
00:06:02,940 --> 00:06:11,350
 Xenodotis, I never sell my books, but I'll be pleased to lend them.

71
00:06:11,410 --> 00:06:12,750
 You might have them copied.

72
00:06:17,690 --> 00:06:24,380
 See, people will be talking about my library.

73
00:06:25,630 --> 00:06:28,490
 Come and see how they're copying them.

74
00:06:28,610 --> 00:06:28,890
 Uh-huh.

75
00:06:29,380 --> 00:06:31,510
 I think you'll be very pleased.

76
00:06:33,080 --> 00:06:38,540
 Keep the originals and give back copies.

77
00:06:39,180 --> 00:06:41,000
 Yes, we'd much rather do that.

78
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:48,800
 Have over 400,000 books here, sire.

79
00:06:48,940 --> 00:07:11,330
 "All the works of the Greek philosophers and scientists, and the life of Alexander, the sacred books of Egypt, Chaldeans horoscopes, now that's what I call a VBL, a school."

80
00:07:11,330 --> 00:07:14,720
 "Greetings, I am Sestibus, engineer."

81
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:21,850
 "Inventor, look, Ptolemy, hmm, this statue draws a line on papyrus. The line represents passing time, you see."

82
00:07:21,850 --> 00:07:23,450
 "That's marvellous."

83
00:07:23,450 --> 00:07:32,930
 "As you see, water flows regularly and pushes up the floater, which in turn fills up the compartment, which gets heavier, and turns the drum on which the line is drawn."

84
00:07:32,950 --> 00:07:37,190
 Quite ingenious, Estebius. Any other inventions?

85
00:07:37,190 --> 00:07:41,790
 This pump, for example, it brings water from a well without lowering a bucket.

86
00:07:41,790 --> 00:07:50,210
 Not only clever, but useful. Go on, what else?

87
00:07:50,210 --> 00:07:54,910
 I am working on this invention, an instrument for measuring any differences in heat.

88
00:07:54,910 --> 00:07:57,020
 Call it a thermometer.

89
00:07:57,020 --> 00:08:00,990
 You see, just the heat of my hands makes the liquid rise in the tube.

90
00:08:00,990 --> 00:08:01,790
 Amusing.

91
00:08:01,790 --> 00:08:03,150
 Run.

92
00:08:03,150 --> 00:08:06,150
 Not sure what this one's going to do.

93
00:08:06,150 --> 00:08:09,980
 The piston compresses the air in the tube, thus creating a force.

94
00:08:09,980 --> 00:08:12,480
 That force? Air doesn't have any force.

95
00:08:12,480 --> 00:08:14,980
 Pull this lever and you'll see if it has any.

96
00:08:14,980 --> 00:08:33,840
 Here, what I intend.

97
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:36,770
 It sure held a lot of water.

98
00:08:36,770 --> 00:08:42,210
 For centuries, the Library of Alexandria was to grow larger.

99
00:08:42,210 --> 00:08:45,830
 Very soon, it would contain more than a million different words.

100
00:08:45,830 --> 00:08:49,330
 Then, in the year '47, before our era.

101
00:08:49,330 --> 00:09:38,970
 It is lost for science.

102
00:09:38,970 --> 00:09:42,430
 Some of the greatest texts went up in flames.

103
00:09:42,430 --> 00:09:44,400
 And all the scientists were killed?

104
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:45,780
 Rome would spare them.

105
00:09:45,780 --> 00:09:46,280
 Oh, good.

106
00:09:46,280 --> 00:09:49,500
 For another two centuries, Alexandria was a province of Rome,

107
00:09:49,500 --> 00:09:54,320
 Rome and was to stay the scientific center of the world. I'll tell you about two

108
00:09:54,320 --> 00:09:58,640
 of those scientists. Ptolemy, whose maps of the sky were the standard for many

109
00:09:58,640 --> 00:10:04,290
 centuries, and Hero. Who knows about Hero of Alexandria? He was surely a genius. One

110
00:10:04,290 --> 00:10:09,440
 of the most remarkable inventors of all time. You see, Hero, the Earth's round and

111
00:10:09,440 --> 00:10:13,900
 the Sun goes around it. The Earth is round, I agree, Ptolemy. But are you sure the

112
00:10:13,900 --> 00:10:17,980
 Sun goes around? The Sun and all the planets move around Earth. I even worked

113
00:10:17,980 --> 00:10:25,050
 out their orbits it's all very complicated how to consider it greetings are you new here yes we are

114
00:10:25,050 --> 00:10:30,090
 scientists from rome my name is tinius i am an engineer he is bought as an astronomer we came

115
00:10:30,090 --> 00:10:35,960
 to see how you work here perhaps we can give you a hand i too am an engineer my friend here ptolemy

116
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:42,450
 is an astronomer and geographer crazy thing it looks like the earth's round the earth is round

117
00:10:42,450 --> 00:10:48,830
 it's a sphere the earth's as flat as a millstone we all know that you've only to look around you

118
00:10:48,830 --> 00:10:52,350
 Guys, where do you come from? These days it's well known the Earth is round.

119
00:10:52,850 --> 00:10:58,030
 We are Romans, and Rome is the strongest, so if we say the Earth's flat, then that's the way it is.

120
00:10:58,760 --> 00:11:01,690
 Your empty head's going to be flat if you go on talking them.

121
00:11:01,710 --> 00:11:04,130
 Don't go on. It's obvious they're backwards.

122
00:11:06,560 --> 00:11:09,110
 You'll find me, Shower.

123
00:11:09,450 --> 00:11:11,950
 If you're a scientist, what have you invented?

124
00:11:12,330 --> 00:11:13,110
 Wanna know?

125
00:11:17,900 --> 00:11:21,440
 A cup and ball game. An old Greek idea. Goes back to Plato's time.

126
00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:25,940
 And you, hero of Alexandria, you say you're an engineer. What did you invent?

127
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:31,040
 Oh, a few gadgets, you could say. Lately, a working steam machine.

128
00:11:31,040 --> 00:11:33,040
 A steam machine? What's that?

129
00:11:33,040 --> 00:11:34,140
 Here, I'll show you.

130
00:11:34,140 --> 00:11:37,460
 That's not a machine.

131
00:11:37,460 --> 00:11:39,260
 No, watch this.

132
00:11:39,260 --> 00:11:56,670
 The mixture of the steam that forces it around. You see, it works.

133
00:11:56,670 --> 00:11:59,030
 Yes, only it doesn't do anything.

134
00:11:59,030 --> 00:12:02,150
 The principle of a steam machine could have a great many uses.

135
00:12:15,580 --> 00:12:18,720
 I destroyed your machine.

136
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:20,990
 Oh, I'll make another one, even more advanced.

137
00:12:21,690 --> 00:12:25,790
 We insist on seeing Caius, governor of Alexandria.

138
00:12:27,900 --> 00:12:31,940
 So, you're playing against the Greeks in the museum at, er...

139
00:12:31,940 --> 00:12:35,790
 Yes, Caius, especially one named Hero. He damages equipment.

140
00:12:36,270 --> 00:12:38,910
 The other, Ptolemy, he maintains the earth is round.

141
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:40,560
 And what is the earth?

142
00:12:40,850 --> 00:12:41,370
 Square.

143
00:12:42,410 --> 00:12:43,440
 Naturally.

144
00:12:44,080 --> 00:12:46,120
 Naturally. Let us go and see the Greeks.

145
00:12:51,110 --> 00:12:57,550
 I am Caius, Governor of Alexandria. Apparently you have invented a steam device.

146
00:12:59,230 --> 00:13:01,160
 Yes, they destroyed it.

147
00:13:01,780 --> 00:13:05,700
 No? And just what use is, er, your thing?

148
00:13:06,020 --> 00:13:08,000
 It's to master the energy in heat.

149
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,940
 Look, either you demonstrate your machine is a useful purpose,

150
00:13:11,940 --> 00:13:15,940
 or else you'll find yourself usefully rowing a Roman galley.

151
00:13:15,940 --> 00:13:17,940
 Oh, hello.

152
00:13:17,940 --> 00:13:20,940
 I am Caius, Roman governor of Alexandria. You must show respect.

153
00:13:20,940 --> 00:13:23,380
 But I respect everyone.

154
00:13:23,380 --> 00:13:26,490
 What are you doing?

155
00:13:26,490 --> 00:13:28,490
 It's a map of the known world.

156
00:13:28,490 --> 00:13:33,150
 And Rome, where is Rome on your map?

157
00:13:33,150 --> 00:13:41,730
 Your map is no good. Rome is at the center of the world, not a speck among all the others.

158
00:13:41,730 --> 00:13:43,980
 That depends on how you look at it.

159
00:13:43,980 --> 00:13:45,890
 That's how I look at it.

160
00:13:45,890 --> 00:13:48,150
 Now listen, you two are a nuisance.

161
00:13:48,150 --> 00:13:51,150
 You will make a machine that does something useful.

162
00:13:51,150 --> 00:13:54,660
 You draw a map with Rome at the center.

163
00:13:54,660 --> 00:13:57,660
 Otherwise, you're going to be pulling ores.

164
00:13:57,660 --> 00:14:05,670
 To do whatever a Roman says.

165
00:14:05,670 --> 00:14:20,900
 Said that the project would get underway, and so you will dig here.

166
00:14:20,900 --> 00:14:25,640
 I really hope your device works.

167
00:14:25,640 --> 00:14:27,760
 Nothing to worry about, you'll see.

168
00:14:27,760 --> 00:14:31,580
 Well, hero, have you done anything useful with your steam machine?

169
00:14:32,960 --> 00:14:35,420
 Is it useful to open the door of this temple?

170
00:14:35,420 --> 00:14:40,250
 With your machine, it's the spagnett, stupid.

171
00:14:40,250 --> 00:14:50,330
 See the opening doors.

172
00:14:50,330 --> 00:15:04,170
 See for yourself.

173
00:15:04,170 --> 00:15:16,430
 Beginning to think you're a true scientist, hero of Alexandria.

174
00:15:16,430 --> 00:15:19,430
 No, no.

175
00:15:19,430 --> 00:15:21,090
 The doors are now going to close up.

176
00:15:21,090 --> 00:15:24,960
 You can try to restrain them.

177
00:15:24,960 --> 00:15:32,910
 It's the machine to let you go.

178
00:15:32,910 --> 00:15:34,970
 You are clever, hero.

179
00:15:34,970 --> 00:15:40,540
 Go on inventing machines that are just as clever as this one is.

180
00:15:40,540 --> 00:15:44,470
 And if you were mapped with Rome at the center?

181
00:15:44,470 --> 00:15:47,330
 I see, I see.

182
00:15:47,330 --> 00:15:50,530
 You see, this is the Colosseum, the circus in Rome.

183
00:15:50,530 --> 00:15:52,050
 It's right in the middle, right?

184
00:15:52,850 --> 00:15:54,730
 And where's the rest of the world?

185
00:15:54,890 --> 00:15:59,370
 Well, according to what you said, Rome is so large and the rest of the world is so small, why even show it?

186
00:16:00,430 --> 00:16:01,790
 And that?

187
00:16:02,650 --> 00:16:06,180
 There are the sun and the moon revolving around the Coliseum.

188
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:07,150
 Hmm.

189
00:16:07,820 --> 00:16:12,070
 As for that, it's all Greek to me. Is he trying to put one over on me?

190
00:16:12,210 --> 00:16:13,790
 Of course he is. He's not serious.

191
00:16:14,170 --> 00:16:17,650
 The earth is flat. Greeks are making fun of us.

192
00:16:17,910 --> 00:16:22,560
 You two are getting on my nerves. If you're a scientist, then prove it, or it's to the galley.

193
00:16:23,120 --> 00:16:26,700
 Good job. How'd you manage to open up the temple doors like that?

194
00:16:26,700 --> 00:16:36,690
 It was easy. Fire heats the air trapped in an airtight container, increasing the air pressure, forcing out the water, through the tube, into an enclosed container.

195
00:16:36,690 --> 00:16:40,690
 The added weight pulls on the rope connected to the doors, which then opens.

196
00:16:40,690 --> 00:16:45,720
 When the fire is extinguished, pressure reduces in the airtight container and water draws back in.

197
00:16:45,720 --> 00:16:47,720
 The counterweight closes the doors.

198
00:16:47,720 --> 00:16:51,990
 Easy as rolling off a log. Quite simple.

199
00:16:56,300 --> 00:17:00,970
 Caius, I must have more men. It's too heavy and we'll never make it.

200
00:17:00,970 --> 00:17:02,970
 I'll send you additional slaves.

201
00:17:02,970 --> 00:17:05,970
 It's not more men who are needed. It's levers that are needed.

202
00:17:05,970 --> 00:17:08,940
 A few men will be able to lift very great weights.

203
00:17:08,940 --> 00:17:12,610
 Roman engineers have interesting ideas.

204
00:17:12,610 --> 00:17:24,690
 It needs to lift it only two feet. We have to bring it up thirty feet.

205
00:17:24,690 --> 00:17:25,690
 Tell me how you'll do that.

206
00:17:25,690 --> 00:17:29,100
 With other levers, pendier but higher and higher.

207
00:17:29,100 --> 00:17:31,120
 How do you intend supporting all of them?

208
00:17:31,120 --> 00:17:35,780
 With much stronger beams, get the idea?

209
00:17:35,780 --> 00:17:50,450
 Your system, each stone is going to take all day.

210
00:17:50,450 --> 00:17:53,180
 All right, you're good for nothings. What are you waiting for?

211
00:17:53,180 --> 00:18:00,860
 That marble is real Carrari. It cost a lot. I will find you the next time.

212
00:18:00,860 --> 00:18:02,590
 Me hero.

213
00:18:02,590 --> 00:18:04,960
 Hello, kids. I see there's a problem.

214
00:18:04,960 --> 00:18:07,040
 Oh, hmm.

215
00:18:07,040 --> 00:18:12,380
 The solution. As it happens, I've just invented the Barakos.

216
00:18:12,380 --> 00:18:13,050
 The what?

217
00:18:13,050 --> 00:18:14,240
 What's that?

218
00:18:14,240 --> 00:18:15,360
 You'll see.

219
00:18:15,360 --> 00:18:20,830
 Now look, I turn the big crank, which in turn puts the cog wheel into motion.

220
00:18:20,830 --> 00:18:24,930
 A smaller wheel transmits the movement, which has much more force, and so on.

221
00:18:24,930 --> 00:18:25,800
 That's it!

222
00:18:25,800 --> 00:18:29,300
 At the other side of the Barakos, the pulley moves very slowly.

223
00:18:29,300 --> 00:18:31,100
 That's where we attach the ropes.

224
00:18:31,100 --> 00:18:32,890
 First, I need a tower.

225
00:18:32,890 --> 00:18:40,650
 Batas!

226
00:18:40,650 --> 00:18:41,850
 Yes, Tignis?

227
00:18:41,850 --> 00:18:44,130
 Batas, you know what you're doing.

228
00:18:44,130 --> 00:18:45,950
 Cranking the machine, Tignis.

229
00:18:45,950 --> 00:18:48,220
 Turning all the wheels, Batas.

230
00:18:48,220 --> 00:18:49,700
 Nicely put, Tignis.

231
00:18:49,700 --> 00:18:52,260
 And you're crushing my finger, Batas.

232
00:18:52,260 --> 00:18:53,160
 Oh, oh!

233
00:19:00,210 --> 00:19:02,910
 Didn't give it a pep.

234
00:19:04,500 --> 00:19:06,330
 He's going for jogging.

235
00:19:06,330 --> 00:19:10,940
 The tower's ready.

236
00:19:10,940 --> 00:19:35,730
 They certainly know what they're doing.

237
00:19:35,730 --> 00:19:43,200
 Hero, I think I found a way to draw more accurate maps. Watch.

238
00:19:43,200 --> 00:19:49,690
 Actually, Ptolemy's maps contain certain errors.

239
00:19:49,690 --> 00:19:53,420
 But all the same, he is the father of modern geography.

240
00:19:53,420 --> 00:19:56,520
 Fourteen centuries later, Christopher Columbus used his original maps,

241
00:19:56,520 --> 00:20:00,160
 and fifteen centuries later, Copernicus would still be under his influence.

242
00:20:00,160 --> 00:20:01,200
 The astrolabe.

243
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:04,300
 What about this instrument for observing the sky?

244
00:20:04,300 --> 00:20:06,400
 Hero, you're not listening. What are you doing?

245
00:20:06,400 --> 00:20:07,740
 Observing, as you say.

246
00:20:07,740 --> 00:20:10,920
 Passing scenery.

247
00:20:10,920 --> 00:20:12,860
 That's my cousin, Clea.

248
00:20:12,860 --> 00:20:17,150
 Well, I was looking at the lyre she's playing.

249
00:20:17,150 --> 00:20:18,920
 Come on, it's time you met her.

250
00:20:27,930 --> 00:20:31,330
 Hello, cousin. I'd like you to meet my friend, Hiro.

251
00:20:31,330 --> 00:20:34,170
 He's an engineer. He'll invent anything you want.

252
00:20:34,170 --> 00:20:37,310
 Hello, Clea. I... well, I... really, I'm...

253
00:20:37,310 --> 00:20:39,290
 Me too, Hiro.

254
00:20:39,290 --> 00:20:41,790
 Very nice music you play on that thing.

255
00:20:41,790 --> 00:20:43,390
 It's a lyre.

256
00:20:43,390 --> 00:20:45,950
 Well, you play on it by plucking the strings.

257
00:20:45,950 --> 00:20:47,810
 But she's found a better way.

258
00:20:47,810 --> 00:20:50,190
 It's prettier when you strike the strings lightly.

259
00:20:50,190 --> 00:20:51,650
 Yes, a good idea.

260
00:20:51,650 --> 00:20:55,550
 You'll see, Clea. He'll come up with a new invention for you.

261
00:20:55,550 --> 00:21:07,000
 I go to jail for defacing Camel.

262
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,180
 A brand new Camel, never been ridden.

263
00:21:09,180 --> 00:21:11,810
 The more I think about it, the angrier I get.

264
00:21:11,810 --> 00:21:13,870
 You buy him!

265
00:21:13,870 --> 00:21:15,800
 Now, now...

266
00:21:15,800 --> 00:21:19,020
 Here are a few coins. Give the Camel a washjob.

267
00:21:19,020 --> 00:21:21,000
 My friend's a bit distracted.

268
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:22,620
 Don't worry.

269
00:21:22,620 --> 00:21:26,370
 In case you get another message, there's the other side of the Camel.

270
00:21:26,370 --> 00:21:27,900
 Sicking his head.

271
00:21:27,900 --> 00:21:32,590
 Isn't your friend a bit odd? He left just like that without word.

272
00:21:32,590 --> 00:21:36,450
 That's how he is when he's an idea. He's lost in his thoughts, but he's nice.

273
00:21:36,450 --> 00:21:37,600
 You want to see him again?

274
00:21:37,600 --> 00:21:39,680
 Might run into each other.

275
00:21:39,680 --> 00:21:49,050
 It's so nice. What is it?

276
00:21:49,050 --> 00:21:55,100
 It's a bit like a lyre or a harp, but with small hammers that come down upon the strings.

277
00:21:55,100 --> 00:21:59,060
 I did it for... you said the other day. Anyway, I...

278
00:21:59,060 --> 00:21:59,710
 Guess what?

279
00:21:59,710 --> 00:22:01,790
 It was for you that I invented it.

280
00:22:01,790 --> 00:22:04,190
 Thank you, hero.

281
00:22:04,190 --> 00:22:14,240
 Oh, Claire, quite nice.

282
00:22:16,190 --> 00:22:54,650
 Interest in life is inventing, and gets old, even his new wife.

283
00:22:54,650 --> 00:23:13,070
 Be among the last scientists of the school of Alexandria.

284
00:23:13,070 --> 00:23:15,070
 The glorious period of Greek science

285
00:23:15,070 --> 00:23:17,160
 that lasts only a thousand years.

286
00:23:17,160 --> 00:23:21,120
 The magnificent library of Alexandria was pillaged by the Romans.

287
00:23:21,120 --> 00:23:26,200
 The powerful Roman Empire in its turn was to fall.

288
00:23:26,200 --> 00:23:32,770
 In 391, when Alexandria was part of the Byzantine Empire...

289
00:23:32,770 --> 00:23:35,990
 Destroy the library and all the workrooms!

290
00:23:35,990 --> 00:23:37,490
 It's a place of heat!

291
00:23:37,490 --> 00:23:50,690
 The disappearance of the Greek school, but its discoveries form the basis of our science today.

292
00:23:51,200 --> 00:23:57,230
 Because the torch of knowledge will be picked up, other scientists will live adventures just as exciting.

293
00:23:57,930 --> 00:23:59,690
 You'll see, you'll see.

